    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Phillips County was named after rancher and state senator Benjamin D. Phillips.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phillips_County%2C_Montana"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The UL Bend National Wildlife Refuge lies deep in the Missouri River Breaks along Phillips County’s southern border, offering a rugged mountain experience.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://phillco.org/living-here/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Anglers in Phillips County chase walleye, historic paddlefish, and trout in Nelson Reservoir, the Missouri River, and local ponds.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://phillco.org/living-here/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Sleeping Buffalo Hot Springs, discovered in the late 1920s, began as the Legion Health Resort and remains a historic mineral spa.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://phillco.org/living-here/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Before 1915, Phillips County was part of Blaine County, and before 1912 it belonged to Chouteau County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phillips_County%2C_Montana"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Phillips County ranks as Montana’s second‑largest by land area and third‑largest overall, covering more than 4,000 square miles.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
